@flexiarg or3/student-project-as-collaboration
@title Treat the Student Project as a Real Collaboration
@audience supervisors, module leads, research centre directors, CPD leads
@tone formal-analytic
@style design-pattern
@sigils [🎓/学 🔗/连]
@see or3/authorship-for-first-time-contributors, or3/teach-the-process-not-the-facility

! conclusion: Treat the Student Project as a Real Collaboration

  + context: A taught student is about to do a piece of work that overlaps with your team's research.

  + IF:
    A postgraduate or professional student — on an MBA, MSc, or CPD route — proposes a project that touches your area, or brings professional expertise your team lacks.

  + HOWEVER:
    Taught projects are institutionally framed as assessment. The dissertation is marked, filed, and forgotten; the student graduates; the analysis is redone later by someone else. Expertise the student already had — in finance, management, service delivery — is treated as incidental to their being a student.

  + THEN:
    Treat the project as a collaboration from the start: co-design the question so the result is usable, give it your team's methods, and plan the output as a joint publication with the student as an author. Then keep the relationship after submission — embed what they built into modules, and involve them in delivering it. Students who are already staff or practitioners are especially worth this, because their professional role gives the work somewhere to land.

  + BECAUSE:
    It is the cheapest research collaboration available and the only one where the collaborator arrives with a supervisor, a deadline, and a reason to do the work well. It converts assessment effort into published evidence and embedded practice, and it builds internal capacity: today's student is often tomorrow's colleague in a position to take the work forward.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[When approving project topics, flag the ones that overlap live research and route them to the relevant team.]
    next[Agree authorship expectations with the student at the start, not at submission — see or3/authorship-for-first-time-contributors.]
